WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.797 --> 00:00:11.091
Fossil fuels have got to go.

00:00:12.342 --> 00:00:16.179
We must put an end to these
climate-destructive, loosely regulated,

00:00:16.179 --> 00:00:18.807
dirty businesses
that are destroying the Earth.

00:00:19.224 --> 00:00:20.517
We've done the math,

00:00:20.559 --> 00:00:21.726
we have the data,

00:00:21.726 --> 00:00:23.645
and we know that these dirty businesses

00:00:23.645 --> 00:00:27.399
are making it increasingly
more uninhabitable on this planet.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:32.195
But when those jobs go away,
there'll be green jobs, right?

00:00:32.821 --> 00:00:34.990
Again, we've run the numbers,
we have the data

00:00:35.031 --> 00:00:38.285
and we know that the more we invest
in a green economy,

00:00:38.326 --> 00:00:39.744
the more jobs there’ll be:

00:00:40.161 --> 00:00:43.790
[more] healthier, higher-paying jobs
in the future than there are today.

00:00:44.374 --> 00:00:46.042
But here's the thing.

00:00:46.751 --> 00:00:48.962
People eat bread, not data.

00:00:49.421 --> 00:00:52.507
And when a corporation
shuts down a coal mine,

00:00:52.549 --> 00:00:55.969
as happy as some of us may be
about cleaner air,

00:00:56.011 --> 00:00:59.014
the reality is that people lose paychecks,

00:00:59.014 --> 00:01:00.307
they lose pensions,

00:01:00.348 --> 00:01:03.894
they lose jobs that they thought
were going to be there for their children.

00:01:03.894 --> 00:01:06.938
Schools close, small businesses shutter,

00:01:06.980 --> 00:01:08.773
and homes are lost.

00:01:08.773 --> 00:01:10.066
Poverty sets in.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:13.361
There are no green jobs
waiting for those folks.

00:01:13.361 --> 00:01:14.779
Shutting down a coal mine,

00:01:14.821 --> 00:01:18.241
a coal refinery
or most polluting facilities

00:01:18.283 --> 00:01:20.827
can devastate a community and a family.

00:01:21.953 --> 00:01:23.121
At the end of the day,

00:01:23.163 --> 00:01:25.707
I'm not so much a tree hugger
as I am a people hugger.

00:01:25.707 --> 00:01:27.959
And I don't really believe
in this dichotomy

00:01:28.001 --> 00:01:29.836
of jobs versus the environment.

00:01:29.878 --> 00:01:31.379
We can protect both.

00:01:31.421 --> 00:01:34.257
But we're not going to get it right
if we just helicopter in

00:01:34.257 --> 00:01:35.800
and tell people what's right.

00:01:35.842 --> 00:01:37.427
That's where unions come in.

00:01:38.303 --> 00:01:42.807
Unions for generations have been fighting
to protect workers' rights and justice.

00:01:44.601 --> 00:01:47.687
I grew up hearing people speak
about justice all the time.

00:01:47.729 --> 00:01:51.566
My mom started the first environmental
justice organization here in Detroit.

00:01:52.442 --> 00:01:55.570
Yes, Donele Wilkins, Detroiters
Working for Environmental Justice.

00:01:55.612 --> 00:01:56.738
(Applause)

00:01:57.572 --> 00:02:00.200
She started the first environmental
justice organization

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:01.618
here in the city of Detroit,

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:05.997
and yet, I still didn't truly understand
the intricacies of workers

00:02:06.039 --> 00:02:07.707
and the environmental movement

00:02:07.749 --> 00:02:10.877
until I was sitting offstage,
listening to a heated back and forth

00:02:10.919 --> 00:02:16.216
between a high-ranking member
of the United Mine Workers of America

00:02:16.257 --> 00:02:18.802
and a panel of climate experts
that I had assembled

00:02:18.802 --> 00:02:22.222
for the Coalition of Black Trade
Unionists's Annual Convention.

00:02:22.931 --> 00:02:27.268
On this panel, I had Cecil Corbin-Mark,
the late Cecil Corbin-Mark,

00:02:27.268 --> 00:02:30.355
deputy director of WE ACT,
an Environmental Justice Organization,

00:02:30.397 --> 00:02:31.773
based out of Harlem;

00:02:31.773 --> 00:02:33.566
Jackie Patterson,

00:02:33.566 --> 00:02:36.569
the founder and director
of the Shirley Chisholm Legacy Project,

00:02:36.611 --> 00:02:38.822
a climate justice resource hub;

00:02:38.822 --> 00:02:41.866
and Donele Wilkins,
CEO of Green Door Initiative,

00:02:41.908 --> 00:02:44.202
who also happens to be my mother.

00:02:44.828 --> 00:02:48.373
(Applause)

00:02:48.373 --> 00:02:50.375
I thought everything was going well.

00:02:50.375 --> 00:02:52.210
I thought that regardless of politics,

00:02:52.252 --> 00:02:56.840
we all kind of agreed that climate change
was a problem for human health,

00:02:56.840 --> 00:02:58.800
for our families, for our homes.

00:02:59.592 --> 00:03:03.972
So there I was, sitting in the first row,
Cecil was moderating,

00:03:04.014 --> 00:03:07.475
and then this high-ranking leader
from the United Mine Workers of America

00:03:07.517 --> 00:03:08.977
comes to the mic.

00:03:10.020 --> 00:03:12.522
And shit got heated.

00:03:12.522 --> 00:03:13.815
(Laughter)

00:03:13.857 --> 00:03:15.358
Like, really bad.

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:17.986
I'm talking at this point,
I'm flushed red,

00:03:17.986 --> 00:03:20.196
sweat is beading down my face,

00:03:20.196 --> 00:03:22.574
and I'm thinking how best
to update my resume

00:03:22.574 --> 00:03:25.618
because they're not going to let me
keep my job after this shit.

00:03:25.618 --> 00:03:26.828
(Laughter)

00:03:26.828 --> 00:03:30.206
I look to my boss and he has
this look on his face that says,

00:03:30.206 --> 00:03:31.916
"Get your ass on stage."

00:03:33.251 --> 00:03:36.671
That's a lot to say in the book,
but I promise you this look said a lot.

00:03:36.713 --> 00:03:38.006
(Laughter)

00:03:38.048 --> 00:03:40.759
When the panel ended, my boss
pulled me aside and he said,

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:42.469
"You need to fix this."

00:03:42.969 --> 00:03:46.139
So I quickly secured a room
to have a private conversation

00:03:46.181 --> 00:03:48.391
with the mineworkers and the panelists,

00:03:48.433 --> 00:03:49.684
and we listened.

00:03:50.727 --> 00:03:53.772
We heard the coal miners
talk about their desires,

00:03:53.813 --> 00:03:57.609
their fears, their legacies,
this dignity in their work.

00:03:58.193 --> 00:04:01.446
And we talked about the real reason
why the coal industry was dying,

00:04:01.488 --> 00:04:08.453
which had more to do with profitability
than it did with activists or policy.

00:04:09.287 --> 00:04:11.623
Coal is becoming more expensive

00:04:11.664 --> 00:04:14.542
while the cost of renewable
energy is falling.

00:04:15.251 --> 00:04:18.838
Investors are divesting from coal
because of its impact on the climate

00:04:18.838 --> 00:04:21.758
and the risk associated
with climate change.

00:04:22.592 --> 00:04:23.968
We all felt heard.

00:04:23.968 --> 00:04:25.553
We felt understood.

00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:29.849
The coal miners had opportunity
to separate the person

00:04:29.849 --> 00:04:31.059
from the problem.

00:04:31.476 --> 00:04:34.062
We talked about the history
of environmental justice

00:04:34.104 --> 00:04:36.106
and its roots in the labor movement.

00:04:37.148 --> 00:04:38.525
Since that day,

00:04:38.566 --> 00:04:40.276
and the months and years following,

00:04:40.276 --> 00:04:42.487
I started to see change
in the coal miners.

00:04:43.947 --> 00:04:48.785
They, for a long time,
have been actively [in] opposition

00:04:48.827 --> 00:04:50.829
to any types of climate policy.

00:04:50.829 --> 00:04:54.165
But now they're open to exploring taxes

00:04:54.207 --> 00:04:56.751
and emission standards
to regulate their industry.

00:04:57.544 --> 00:05:01.131
And while they aren't exactly
where I would like them to be,

00:05:01.131 --> 00:05:03.800
they have come a long way,
they've made progress.

00:05:04.926 --> 00:05:08.054
Unions have a long history
of keeping the needs

00:05:08.096 --> 00:05:10.932
and priorities of workers in their mind,

00:05:10.974 --> 00:05:13.393
protecting workers rights
and fighting for justice.

00:05:13.393 --> 00:05:15.520
In Memphis, 1968,

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:18.648
a union of sanitation workers
went on strike

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:21.025
after two African Americans
had been crushed

00:05:21.025 --> 00:05:23.528
in a malfunctioning garbage truck.

00:05:25.071 --> 00:05:28.616
That strike ended in April of 1968,

00:05:28.616 --> 00:05:32.537
when the city of Memphis agreed
to recognize their union

00:05:32.537 --> 00:05:34.664
and to meet the workers' demands.

00:05:34.706 --> 00:05:36.624
In South Africa in 1985,

00:05:36.624 --> 00:05:39.085
the Congress of South African
Trade Unionists

00:05:39.085 --> 00:05:42.672
quickly became the strongest
and most powerful union in South Africa

00:05:42.714 --> 00:05:46.009
by putting pressure on the apartheid
government through protests,

00:05:46.050 --> 00:05:47.760
boycotts and strikes

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:50.597
that led to negotiations in 1990

00:05:50.597 --> 00:05:52.932
and the end of apartheid in 1994.

00:05:53.433 --> 00:05:55.185
And in Denmark,

00:05:55.185 --> 00:05:59.689
when the country was transitioning
away from fossil fuels to wind energy,

00:05:59.689 --> 00:06:02.942
the union 3F negotiated
with the government

00:06:02.984 --> 00:06:06.362
and wind energy companies to secure jobs,

00:06:06.404 --> 00:06:09.032
retraining programs and fair wages.

00:06:09.490 --> 00:06:12.452
Those workers were equipped
to get employed

00:06:12.493 --> 00:06:15.163
in this new and growing
wind energy sector.

00:06:20.043 --> 00:06:22.921
Those workers were equipped.

00:06:24.964 --> 00:06:26.633
You know,

00:06:26.674 --> 00:06:30.470
the union movement is the only
multigenerational, multiethnic,

00:06:30.511 --> 00:06:32.889
multiracial, multigender
movement in the world.

00:06:32.931 --> 00:06:35.767
We're talking about tens
of millions of people

00:06:35.808 --> 00:06:38.519
united around a singular set of ideas.

00:06:38.561 --> 00:06:41.356
So it makes so much sense
that they're a part of this fight

00:06:41.356 --> 00:06:42.565
for a just transition.

00:06:43.024 --> 00:06:45.401
So how can we,

00:06:45.443 --> 00:06:49.030
those of us that are in the climate fight,
properly work with unions?

00:06:49.447 --> 00:06:51.532
It starts with deep listening
conversations,

00:06:51.574 --> 00:06:54.202
like the ones we had
at CBTU's annual convention.

00:06:54.744 --> 00:07:01.125
And workers and those who are pushing
for a green energy transition

00:07:01.125 --> 00:07:02.585
need to hear each other out.

00:07:03.127 --> 00:07:06.422
We need to hear each other's fears,
each other's desires,

00:07:06.464 --> 00:07:08.174
each other's ideas for the future.

00:07:09.384 --> 00:07:11.928
There needs to be
an education on both sides,

00:07:11.970 --> 00:07:15.223
one that allows us to learn
about the livelihood of workers

00:07:15.223 --> 00:07:18.851
and for all of us to learn
about the impact of the climate crisis.

00:07:18.851 --> 00:07:22.480
Since that conversation
that I had at CBTU,

00:07:22.522 --> 00:07:25.650
I started to actively invite
the coal miners to meetings

00:07:25.692 --> 00:07:29.112
and to the conferences that I attend
discussing just transition.

00:07:30.029 --> 00:07:34.742
My desire is for them to articulate
a path towards green jobs

00:07:34.784 --> 00:07:40.957
before negative profitability and policies
decides the fate for them.

00:07:41.708 --> 00:07:45.086
I want unions to secure federal dollars

00:07:45.128 --> 00:07:48.089
to aid in this transition
to retrain their members.

00:07:48.965 --> 00:07:51.217
I want them to realize
they don't have to choose

00:07:51.259 --> 00:07:55.596
between making a living and living
in a safe and healthy environment.

00:07:55.638 --> 00:07:56.973
They can have both.

00:07:56.973 --> 00:07:58.891
Hell, they deserve both.

00:08:00.601 --> 00:08:04.439
(Applause)

00:08:04.439 --> 00:08:07.525
I want them to realize
that they can live in a community

00:08:07.525 --> 00:08:10.361
that generates renewable energy,

00:08:10.361 --> 00:08:13.573
that they can live in a community
where they don't have to speculate

00:08:13.614 --> 00:08:15.366
what's in their water and their air.

00:08:15.408 --> 00:08:19.120
A community where people
are valued over pollution.

00:08:20.330 --> 00:08:21.956
I want them -- thank you.

00:08:22.415 --> 00:08:23.624
(Laughs)

00:08:23.666 --> 00:08:25.835
(Applause)

00:08:25.877 --> 00:08:30.048
I want them to realize that this isn't
some distant utopic future,

00:08:30.089 --> 00:08:33.176
but a reality that can be had
in a few short years.

00:08:33.926 --> 00:08:37.347
If the US government can bail out banks
and the auto industry,

00:08:37.388 --> 00:08:40.224
like they did in the financial
crisis of 2008,

00:08:40.224 --> 00:08:43.144
surely they can bail out
the American worker.

00:08:44.395 --> 00:08:49.776
(Applause)

00:08:49.776 --> 00:08:52.320
But I have a feeling
that this isn't going to happen

00:08:52.362 --> 00:08:54.655
without a concerted push from the unions.

00:08:55.782 --> 00:08:59.577
To transition to a green-energy economy,

00:08:59.619 --> 00:09:01.662
to protect our planet,

00:09:01.704 --> 00:09:03.915
we're going to have to make
some tough changes.

00:09:03.915 --> 00:09:05.541
But I'm a serial optimist,

00:09:05.583 --> 00:09:08.586
and I believe we can make this change
without too much pain.

00:09:08.586 --> 00:09:09.796
Thank you.

00:09:09.796 --> 00:09:11.756
(Applause)

